{
  "note": "Appendix B of the MCS6500 Programming Manual, which gives every instruction its own page: the flags it touches, and for each addressing mode the opcode, the byte count and the cycle count. Read as printed rather than through the extracted text, because the flag row is a line of check marks, dashes and digits that no text layer survives. This is the manufacturer's own statement of the instruction set, so it is what the opcode table and the cycle counts are held to.",
  "document": "mos6500_programming",
  "section": "Appendix B, Instruction List Alphabetic by Mnemonic",
  "printedPages": "B-3 to B-29",
  "pdfPages": "200 to 226",
  "readOn": "2026-08-21",
  "notation": {
    "changes": "The manual prints a check mark: the flag takes a value from the result.",
    "unchanged": "The manual prints a dash: the flag is not touched.",
    "0": "The manual prints a zero: the flag is always reset.",
    "1": "The manual prints a one: the flag is always set.",
    "bit 7 of memory": "The manual prints M with a subscript seven, only on BIT.",
    "bit 6 of memory": "The manual prints M with a subscript six, only on BIT.",
    "from stack": "The manual prints From Stack across the whole row, on PLP and RTI."
  },
  "misprints": {
    "note": "Six of the eight Group One instructions print an asterisk on their indirect indexed row, for the cycle a page crossing adds. Two do not, and the part adds the cycle on all eight.",
    "missingPageCrossingMark": ["AND", "ORA"],
    "carryIt": ["ADC", "CMP", "EOR", "LDA", "SBC"],
    "why": "The asterisk is printed for AND and ORA on their absolute indexed rows on the same page, so the omission is on the one row rather than a claim about the instruction. Nothing distinguishes these two from the six that carry it."
  },
